:: StoryTitle
The Drifting Continents

:: StoryData
{
  "format": "SugarCube",
  "format-version": "2.36.1",
  "start": "Start"
}

:: Start
''New York, 1926.'' You're a young geologist at a big meeting about Alfred Wegener's idea that the continents move. Before anyone votes, look at the case for and against.

[[Look at Wegener's evidence->Evidence]]

:: Evidence
''The fit.'' The edges of South America and Africa fit like puzzle pieces, even better if you match the edges of the shallow sea floor instead of the beaches.
''Fossils.'' Fossils of Mesosaurus, a small freshwater reptile, are found in both Brazil and southern Africa.
''Rocks.'' Rock types and formations on one side of the Atlantic match the other side.
''Ice and coal.'' Scratches from ancient glaciers are found in places that are hot today, like India and Africa. Antarctica has coal made from plants that needed warmth.

[[Hear the objections->Objections]]

:: Objections
''What force?'' Wegener can't say what pushes the continents. The forces he suggests are far too weak.
''Through solid rock?'' How could a continent plow through the hard rock of the ocean floor?
''Too fast.'' Wegener's guess for how fast the continents move is far too fast.

[[Time to decide->Vote1926]]

:: Vote1926
How do you vote?
[[The continents move. Wegener is right.->Voted][$vote1 to "move"]]
[[The continents stay put. The objections win.->Voted][$vote1 to "stay"]]
[[Not sure yet. I need to know what could move them.->Voted][$vote1 to "wait"]]

:: Voted
There's no wrong vote here: smart scientists voted all three ways. Wegener dies in 1930 on an expedition in Greenland. For years, drift looks like a dead idea.

[[Jump ahead to 1952->Rift]]

:: Rift
''1952.'' Marie Tharp, a geologist and mapmaker, is drawing ocean-depth data. Down the middle of a huge underwater mountain range in the Atlantic, she finds a deep valley. What could it mean?
[[The ocean floor is pulling apart there.->RiftReveal]]
[[An ancient river carved it.->RiftReveal]]
[[It's just a random dip.->RiftReveal]]

:: RiftReveal
Tharp thought the valley was a crack where the sea floor was pulling apart. Her research partner Bruce Heezen brushed it off at first. About a year later, earthquake locations lined up along her valley, and he came around.

[[Jump ahead to 1962->Stripes]]

:: Stripes
''1962–1963.'' Harry Hess suggests new sea floor forms at the ridges and spreads outward on both sides. Rocks record which way Earth's magnetism pointed when they formed. If the floor spreads from a ridge, what pattern would the magnetic stripes make?
[[The same stripes on both sides, like a mirror->StripesReveal]]
[[A random jumble->StripesReveal]]
[[Stripes on one side only->StripesReveal]]

:: StripesReveal
It's a mirror. In 1963, Fred Vine and Drummond Matthews said spreading would make the stripes match on both sides of a ridge. By 1966, ship surveys showed they did. By the late 1960s most geologists accept plate tectonics.

[[Vote again->VoteLate]]

:: VoteLate
Do the continents move?
[[Yes->Closed]]
[[No->Closed]]
[[Not sure yet->Closed]]

:: Closed
''Notebook closed.'' The fossils and rocks never changed. What changed was having a mechanism, and a new way of seeing the whole Earth.
